-- Where a book was left off.
--
-- A table of its own rather than columns on `document`, because it has its own
-- lifetime: most books have never been opened, and the ones that have are
-- written to as they are read.
--
-- `pageCount` sits beside the page so how far through a book somebody is can be
-- answered without opening it. The pair means something together and neither
-- half alone: for a PDF they are pages the file has, for a reflowable book pages
-- the reader set in the window and type size it was last read at - so they are
-- written and replaced together, and the same place stores a different count at
-- a different size.
--
-- A foreign key on `document`, cascading. Every route in goes through a book the
-- library already holds, so the row has no life of its own to protect - and
-- leaving the rule to `RemoveDocumentUseCase` meant a process dying between two
-- of its four writes left a position for a book on no shelf, which nothing
-- lists, sweeps or can reach again. The schema owns the rule now.
--
-- Nothing may `INSERT OR REPLACE` into `document`: a replace is a delete and an
-- insert, and with this key that delete takes the reader's place in the book
-- with it - which is why the import is `INSERT OR IGNORE` plus an `UPDATE`.
--
-- `readAt` orders the shelf by what was read last, counting writes rather than
-- telling the time - one past the highest in the table - for
-- `translation_cache.usedAt`'s reason: a device clock can go backwards, and all
-- this must answer is which of two came later.
CREATE TABLE reading_position (
    location TEXT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES document(location) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    pageIndex INTEGER NOT NULL,
    pageCount INTEGER NOT NULL,
    readAt INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
);

selectByLocation:
SELECT * FROM reading_position WHERE location = ?;

-- Every stored position at once, for the library grid.
--
-- One statement rather than one per book: the grid shows how far through each
-- book its reader is, and asking book by book is a query per cell on a shelf of
-- hundreds. The whole table is a handful of numbers per book - less than one
-- cover.
selectAll:
SELECT * FROM reading_position;

-- Replace rather than update: the row holds nothing that is not being written.
-- `INSERT OR REPLACE` is as old as SQLite, unlike the upsert this would
-- otherwise be - see `Document.sq`.
--
-- The subselect runs before the conflicting row is replaced, so a book written
-- again comes out above every other rather than keeping the place it had.
put:
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO reading_position(location, pageIndex, pageCount, readAt)
VALUES (?, ?, ?, (SELECT COALESCE(MAX(readAt), 0) + 1 FROM reading_position));

deleteByLocation:
DELETE FROM reading_position WHERE location = ?;
